    hears nothing about its resolution, which is usually the complaint that prompts building notifications in the first
    place.
  - [1mExplicit watchers[0m — a many-to-many watchers list, users opt in per task. Precise and predictable, no surprise noise.
    Costs a join table and a UI affordance, and in practice people forget to subscribe, so they miss things anyway.
  - [1mImplicit involvement[0m — you're auto-subscribed by acting on a task (created it, was assigned, commented), with an
    explicit mute. Matches what GitHub and Linear do and matches how people actually expect this to behave. Costs the
    most: needs an activity/participation record, and mute state, and it generates the most volume — which makes
    notification preferences mandatory rather than optional.
  - [1mProject/list membership[0m — subscribe to a container, get everything in it. Cheap if tasks are grouped, very noisy if
    they aren't.

  These aren't exclusive; real systems layer implicit involvement over project membership with per-task mute. But
  layering them all is how a notifications system becomes the biggest component in the app, so I'd rather start narrow.

  My recommendation is [1mimplicit involvement with explicit mute[0m. It's what users mean by "tasks I care about" without
  being asked, and the participation record it requires is useful for an activity feed anyway. Explicit watchers is the
  cheap fallback if you want to cut scope — it's a strict subset, so starting there and adding auto-subscribe later is
  not a migration, just a new rule.
